WEBVTT

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.100
Oh

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.970
What a thrill it was to have this long-admired painting by David Shepard

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.680
of a wise old elephant suddenly come to life right before our eyes in one of East Africa's

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:14.160
wildlife parks, which Robbins and Meg Barstow had the fabulous experience of visiting in 1978.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.400
All my life, I have been fascinated by the non-human wildlife on our shared planet.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:26.560
As a young boy, I loved going to zoos like the Bronx Zoo in New York to see wondrous

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.900
animals like this African elephant, even though I felt

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.360
sorry for their being captives behind bars and fences. This was the only way that I could

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.820
encounter them at that time. I nourished the dream of someday being able to see them in their natural

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:48.910
habitat, wild and free. But it was not until our own children were grown up and my wife and I

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:57.860
revisited the historic Bronx Zoo in 1978 that this dream was able to be realized. Robbins and Meg

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.700
Barstow from Wethersfield, Connecticut.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.670
found that the zoo had been expanded and enhanced

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.050
by providing more natural outdoor settings for some of the animals.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.880
We learned that the New York Zoological Society

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.190
was sponsoring a special four-week African wildlife safari trip

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.840
to the countries of Tanzania and Kenya,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.360
where we could at long last see elephants and hippos and rhinos and lions

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.020
in their real natural habitat.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.510
So we seized the day.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.800
started preparing for what was to be for us the supreme experience of a lifetime on planet earth

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:43.330
for me it went back exactly 50 years to a book called safari written in 1928 when i was just

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:49.570
nine years old by osa and martin johnson it had marvelous close-up photos of large african mammals

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:55.060
which i used to trace for my first wildlife notebook another one of my early heroes was

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.400
Carl Ackley, the author of Adventures in the African Jungle.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:02:59.760
Actually,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:06.020
Over more than half a lifetime, we had gathered a huge collection of books about African wildlife,

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.220
which had introduced us not only to the tremendous variety of animals,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:15.360
but also to the magic names of the magnificent locales where these beasts dwelt in the wild.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:22.260
The African Plains, Animals of East Africa, The End of the Game for African Elephants,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.980
Wildlife in Crisis, Endangered Species, An Artist in Africa,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.050
Africa, with great paintings by British artist David Shepard, in the shadow of Kilimanjaro,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.960
Serengeti, Kingdom of Predators, with color photos by renowned naturalist George Scheller,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:54.700
the fervent affirmation that Serengeti shall not die, the African experience by Peter Matheson,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.920
among the elephants by Ian Douglas Hamilton, whose jungle elephant plague.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.690
ground we were actually going to visit. Portraits in the wild by Cynthia Mons, whom we were going

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:10.890
to see and talk with in person at her elephant research camp. And finally, the field guide to

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:16.450
the mammals and birds of East Africa, which we would take with us to help us identify all of the

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:26.020
different species we expected to see. Africa is a large continent, which until after World War II

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.960
consisted mostly of European colonies. The two countries we were

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.840
to visit. Straddling the equator in Central East Africa, Kenya and Tanzania did not gain

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:42.070
their independence until the 1960s. Mount Kilimanjaro is just inside the border of Tanzania.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.520
By 1978, less than 20 years after becoming independent republics, both Tanzania and Kenya

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.380
had strongly committed themselves to preserving their wildlife through an extensive system of

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.840
national parks. We visited four in Tanzania and eight in Kenya. Driving down the Merritt Park

00:04:59.840 --> 00:04:59.940
work.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.380
from our suburban home in Wethersfield, Connecticut,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.320
en route to Kennedy Airport on our July 13th departure date,

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.660
we were filled with eager anticipation.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:15.110
With our suitcases full and with all kinds of photographic and travel paraphernalia,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:20.850
we headed in to board our British Airways 747 jumbo jet.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.690
The first leg of our long intercontinental journey

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.980
took us from New York to London on an overnight flight.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.920
Then, after a brief airport rest, we embarked on an even longer flight across southern Europe

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.440
and then over northern Africa all the way to Kilimanjaro Airport across the equator in Tanzania.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:48.250
We saw the sun rise over the Mediterranean and got a glimpse of the Nile River

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.150
after a refueling stop in Khartoum, Sudan.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.390
Most of the way, we were flying high above the clouds.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:59.850
But it was exciting when, for the first time in our lives,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.020
we crossed the imaginary line of the equator into the southern hemisphere, even at 30,000 feet.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:13.120
As we neared our destination, we experienced the thrill of sighting a distant mountaintop above the clouds.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.460
It was Kilimanjaro, the highest mountain in Africa,

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:25.920
and as we got nearer, its double-peaked profile almost took on the appearance of a giant whale floating on a white sea of clouds.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:29.710
Finally,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.900
The plane descended through the clouds, and we found ourselves skimming above the ground

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:40.440
and making a dramatic landing at the newly opened Kilimanjaro Airport in Tanzania.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:48.110
Just setting foot for the first time on African soil was an emotional experience for both Meg and me.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.900
Here we were at long last about to set forth on our incredible African odyssey.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:59.810
We immediately felt that Tanzania does indeed welcome the world with the Swahili.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.780
reading, Karabuni. After going through customs, the 20 members of our special tour group found

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.840
waiting for us just outside the airport the fleet of mini-buses, which were to be our primary mode

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.100
of transportation for the next four weeks. There were welcoming banners on the roadway as we

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:24.930
left the airport and headed into the countryside. The first animals we saw were domesticated cattle

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:29.970
being herded along the road, but before long our vans left the main highway and headed

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.080
up a long, bumpy dirt road through a variety of rural countryside and farmland to the paradise-like

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:43.840
jungle location where we were to spend our first night in Africa. At the end of the road, we found

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:49.520
ourselves surrounded by lush plants and trees in a green mansion setting, which was the site of the

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:56.230
Mount Murrow Lodge, formerly the headquarters of a large plantation. As we walked across the rustic

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:59.590
bridge over a small stream covered with brightly colored flowers.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:07.050
and climbed the forest stairway to the lodge all our senses were almost overwhelmed by this

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:13.390
awesome atmosphere on the lodge steps we were greeted by the current owner a british engineer

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:19.620
and architect named michael leach who had made this magical mystical place an overnight stopping

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.300
point for tourist groups from around the world on cloudless days we were told one could look

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.740
across the terraced garden in front of the mansion and see Mount Kilimanjaro.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:39.780
beyond the gardens off to the left beside a small pond there was a large tree which was the nesting

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:46.440
site for literally hundreds of beautiful white herons from time to time my heroine would fly

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:58.470
off and then return with a twig or straw to add to its nest later we went out for a short walk

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.980
and we got just glim

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.240
of our first animal life. They were colobus monkeys high in the treetops,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:12.850
leaping from branch to branch almost invisibly. The next morning our group's

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.130
four minibuses started out for our first day of adventures with African

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.460
wildlife. And what a day of it would turn out to be. After a brief visit to the

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:27.800
post office in the small town of Arusha, we headed down the highway toward our

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.960
first national park and suddenly they're in

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:39.030
the distance, we saw our first game, a tall giraffe meandering across an open field. Further down the

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:44.960
road, a pack of wild zebra ran across right in front of us. And then we saw an ostrich trotting

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:51.910
through the grass in this unfenced open countryside. When we saw the first legendary baobab tree, we

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:56.770
immediately understood why they are said to have been planted upside down with their roots in the

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:59.980
sky coming to a

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.140
major crossroads, our excitement grew as we saw the fabled names on the signpost with distances

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:14.890
measured in kilometers. Finally, our vans arrived at the hillside entrance to Lake Manyara National

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:28.720
Park. What an awesome view it offered. Sitting on a rock beside the entrance was a large baboon

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:29.960
greeting us as if...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.560
he were a chamber of commerce representative then up the hill came the biggest thrill so far the

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:43.290
sighting of a lone bull elephant with white tusks flapping his giant ears the first one i had ever

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:51.580
seen in the wild midway up the high escarpment which borders the park we used binoculars to

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:57.980
view the lushly forested hillside and to note the spectacular location of the hotel we would be

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.980
staying at right on the rim of the

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.740
steep slope this spot gave us a panoramic view of the great rift valley which harbors the park's

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:13.260
unique combination of forest woodland grassland swamp and the broad shallow lake which gives the

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:18.700
park its name the hotel was a fine modern building with comfortable accommodations designed

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:24.440
to meet the needs and tastes of tourists from around the world meg and i were especially attired

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:29.800
as we stood outside our hotel room overlooking the vast valley where soon we were to encounter

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.300
the incredible profusion of animal and bird life, which makes Lake Manyara one of the world's

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:41.920
most fabulous wildlife habitats. Before leaving Connecticut, Megan made two special light green

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:48.160
safari jackets with all kinds of extra pockets, which we took great pleasure in wearing throughout

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:54.010
the trip. After meeting the chief park warden, Mr. Benjamin Kansa, the members of our group again

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:58.790
boarded our special vans and set out on our first actual game drive

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.960
guided ride through a designated park area for the express purpose of viewing and photographing

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:11.680
wildlife. The vans we rode in were designed and built just for this purpose. Each vehicle held

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.650
five passengers with three pairs of seats, two in the rear, two in the middle, and one in the front

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:24.100
beside the driver so that every rider had a window seat. Another special feature of these vans was the

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:29.240
large opening in the middle of each roof, which enabled passengers on occasion to stand with

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:29.920
their heads out.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.840
and take unimpeded snapshots, as Meg did for us,

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.000
or to take movies, as I did, when not using binoculars.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.320
Just beyond the entrance gate of the park,

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:43.800
there was a small clearing

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.750
where we were able to observe a family of baboons

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.310
foraging in the grass

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:52.400
and also to get a good view of a warthog

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.690
with its curled tusks and ugly snout.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:58.770
We were intrigued to see it get down on its front knees

00:12:58.770 --> 00:12:59.970
some of the time to nibble.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:08.840
the grass and dig for roots. The first big game we saw on this afternoon's drive was a large

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:16.750
elephant with a broken tusk standing just by itself near the road. We wondered what its life

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:26.140
story might be. Next we came upon a tall giraffe which we recognized as being a Maasai giraffe

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:29.720
as distinguished from the reticulated species of giraffe

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.250
which we would see later in Kenya.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.080
It was accompanied by a smaller, younger giraffe,

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:39.770
which appeared to be its adolescent offspring.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:46.720
Then we saw our first wild rhinoceros off in the middle of a field.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:50.310
We were just as glad not to have it come too close.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:56.380
As we approached the lakeside, we saw a small herd of cape buffalo grazing in the marsh,

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:59.960
and then at the edge of the lake we found a great profusion of birds.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.660
and in the water beyond them a group of hippos, one of them raising its head above

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:10.780
the surface and opening its mammoth mouth. The most beautiful animals we saw on this

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:17.390
first drive were the impala, the most graceful of all African antelopes. The

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:23.370
males have exquisitely curved lyre-like horns. First we watched this bachelor herd

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:28.570
walk by in supreme dignity and grace and then we came across a breeding herd of

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:29.270
females

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.450
which have no horns, kept together by a single male who guarded his harem from a small mound in

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:45.920
the center. We saw many storks and vultures in the tops of the acacia trees as we moved on to another

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:52.800
part of the forest where we were to experience one of our most exciting encounters. Our safari's

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:59.980
first sight of a wild lion was this magnificent black-maned male up in a tree. This was

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.140
the home of Manyara's famed tree-climbing lions, and during the heat of the day, they would often

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:11.020
climb up to these easily accessible roosts to watch the scene below or just sprawl out on a

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:18.340
branch for a rest. We were fascinated watching this impressive king of beasts as he moved around in

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:23.890
his tree branch lair, but when I put my head up through the rooftop opening of our van to get a

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:26.450
better picture. Suddenly he let out a loud roar.

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:41.450
So we rapidly drove off in search of the nearby elephants stamping ground.

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:45.260
When the van stopped here for picture taking,

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:48.050
we found ourselves looking at not just one,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.450
but whole families of wild African elephants.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.510
We were right in the midst of the very elephants

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:57.430
which the Scottish researcher Ian Douglas Hamilton

00:15:57.430 --> 00:15:59.970
had studied in this area for five years.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.440
years. What a thrill it was to be, if only for a short while, as he titled his famous

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:11.960
book Among the Elephants. We hated to leave this weary mother and her very, very young

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:16.940
calf, but we continued on to another part of the park, which seemed to have been taken

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:22.820
over by giraffes. We were impressed by the awkward dignity with which they walked, and

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:28.760
also by how fast they could run. There were many young giraffes here, some lying on the

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:29.920
ground and

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.150
could see that it was no easy task for them to untangle their long legs and stand up as we

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:44.380
wondered what the view must be like from such a height something quite unexpected occurred in the

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:52.060
underbrush beside the road we saw a rhino and then as we watched the giraffes along the side of the

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:59.030
lake, two rhinos came charging in among them, scattering the giraffes in all directions.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.600
This caused all kinds of excitement.

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:09.720
But on the way back, tragically, we came across what apparently had spooked the rhinos.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:16.830
It was the fresh carcass of a large rhinoceros which had very recently been killed by poachers.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.400
We were tremendously shocked at this gruesome sight.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:27.880
Both the brain and the horns of this magnificent beast had been crudely cut out and removed from its blood-covered head.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:29.970
Our driver pointed out the...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.220
bullet holes in the animal's side which had ended its life. Rhino horns bring huge sums on the black

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:42.580
market because of their supposed aphrodisiac qualities, and we now had seen firsthand why

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:48.140
seeking to stop poaching has been one of the most difficult challenges facing the managers of

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:54.060
East Africa's parks. Some of this film footage was later used by the World Wildlife Fund to help

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:59.960
raise money to protect African rhinos, vervet monkeys in the...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.100
trees overhead as we departed Manara National Park seemed to echo our exclamations over what

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:13.900
a first day of African wildlife adventures this had been. Ten different major species of animals

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:20.040
all in one park in one day. It took us another day to recover from our sensory overload and to travel

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:28.220
to our next overnight stop, which was to be the incredible Ngorongoro Crater Lodge. This lodge is

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:29.980
located right on the rim of one of

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.480
the most amazing natural wonders in the world. A gigantic circular crater 2,000 feet deep and 10

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:44.300
miles in diameter, encompassing 100 square miles of flat grassland, woods and lake, which can only

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:50.690
be described as a living Garden of Eden. The crater is what remains of a collapsed ancient

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:57.620
volcano of near record size. Sunrise over the crater is an awesome sight because within the

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.820
The high walls surrounding this cell contain

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.980
equatorial paradise resides one of the most evenly balanced populations of wildlife anywhere

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:14.630
on earth. A large portion of the approximately 25,000 animals who dwell here spend all or most

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:21.000
of their lives on the crater floor where human habitation has been prohibited. All visitors were

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:27.870
required to travel in special Land Rover jeeps and as the vehicles wound their way down the steep

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:29.970
roadways along the cliff side we can

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:59.970
CREATOR'S PREDATORS, AND HERE IS ONE OF THE CHIEF PREDATORS, THE HIITAS.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:11.120
To maintain population stability, there must be thousands of grass-eating herbivores to provide food for a few hundred meat-eating carnivores.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:18.440
And the largest of the predators here is the magnificent lion with its tawny hide blending perfectly with its tall yellow grass.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:23.090
These shots were taken without a telephoto lens.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:29.960
Not far away we saw another smaller prey species, the Thompson's gazelles with the crater walls towering.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.420
behind them. And when we reached the edge of the crater's great lake, on the far side we could see

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:43.640
hippos temporarily out of the water. On a beautiful hillside overlooking the lake, we all stopped for a

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:50.920
picnic lunch with a background of unparalleled scenic grandeur. Tree, water, rocks, grass, we were

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:57.300
almost overwhelmed by the magic enchantment of this fantastic African setting. What a supreme

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.660
example of God's earthly creation.

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:06.770
passing a clump of trees on our way back we had a good opportunity to observe the

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:12.230
distinguishing characteristics of the african elephant it is a different species from the more

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:17.510
readily domesticated indian elephant which has much smaller ears and a high square forehead

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:23.230
african elephants like this fine specimen have very large flapping ears and a rounded forehead

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:29.910
as well as generally larger tusks a pair of ostriches bid us farewell as we

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.560
left Ngorongoro Crater.

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:34.310
En route to our next destination,

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:36.570
we passed a herd of domestic cattle,

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:38.070
which properly have the right-of-way

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.350
in this protected area outside the park.

00:21:40.690 --> 00:21:43.890
We made a brief stop at Old Duvay Gorge,

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.730
the world-famous site where, in 1959,

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.550
Dr. Louis Leakey and his wife Mary

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:52.330
discovered the skull of a human-like creature

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.530
believed to be over 2 million years old.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.120
It was then the earliest known humanoid predecessor

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.720
of present-day human beings.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:29.680
after this our minivans headed cross-country toward the fabled serengeti plains ever since childhood the name serengeti had had a magical sound to it all our lives we had heard and dreamed about this special area in east africa which is the home of the largest numbers of different varieties of wildlife any place on planet earth a superb starling was showing off its brilliantly colored luster when we arrived at the nabby hill entrance gate

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:42.180
Meg and I wanted the thrill of entering on foot, when at long last, for the very first time, we crossed the threshold into the Tanzanian wonderland of Serengeti National Park.

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:50.740
As the bands proceeded past amber fields of grass, we immediately began to encounter large numbers of antelope of different species.

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:55.540
First across the roadway, scampered a troop of black-striped Thompson's gazelles.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:59.500
THEN WE ENCOUNTERED A HEARTOBEAST.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.660
commanding respect for its right of way before moving on and showing off its unique four-pronged

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:12.650
profile with two tall ears sticking out below its curved horns. He seemed to have a family

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:22.500
nearby. Suddenly, a huge giraffe galloped across the road right in front of the van, so close

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:29.980
we could almost touch it. The next species we saw was a kind of antelope called the topi.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.840
We enjoyed watching a pair of young males engaged in some playful head-butting and horn-testing.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:46.580
After an hour-long drive, we were glad to see the welcoming banner announcing our approach to Serenera.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:53.700
Serengeti National Park is a tremendous natural preserve extending up to the Tanzanian-Kenyan border,

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:56.220
and Serenera is its heart.

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:59.980
The park encompasses more than 5,000 square miles.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.260
miles, and it contains the greatest and most spectacular concentration of plains animals

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:10.360
anywhere in the world. They are counted in the millions and include more than 35 different

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:15.880
species. The fabulous Serenera Wildlife Lodge is built right in among a large outcropping

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:21.660
of huge rocks. From the wide picture windows on the veranda of the great high-ceiling dining

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:28.430
room, one looks directly out over the Serengeti plains. By incredible good fortune, our visit

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:29.890
happened to coincide with

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.620
visit from the Tanzanian Minister of National Parks, whom we had the honor to meet and talk

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:41.840
with. Accompanying him was the world-famous international wildlife conservationist Prince

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:48.060
Bernhard of the Netherlands, whom we saw drive off in this jeep for a special game-viewing tour.

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:55.460
Serenera was the base from which the American naturalist and author George Schaller had

00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:59.840
conducted his extensive pioneering field studies of the African lion.

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:04.550
The Serengeti Research Institute had been opened here in 1973

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:08.610
by Tanzania's first president, Julius Nayarera,

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:14.280
and research was now being carried out by a number of native Tanzanians.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:18.240
We were greatly impressed by the work they were doing

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:22.080
and their continuing dedication to the cause of conservation in their country.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:27.440
And these are the animals about which so many books, photographs, films,

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:29.880
and television programs have been made in the years.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.780
since Serengeti became a national park in 1951.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:38.980
In this kingdom of predators, even giraffe occasionally fall prey to the lion.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:43.420
And we found this partially devoured carcass being guarded by a lioness

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:46.660
while others rested nearby and in the trees above.

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:48.740
The vultures awaited their turn.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.020
Then we came across a lion nursery

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:55.700
where a mother was calmly lying in the grass with some young cubs

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.760
and then rolled over on her back with her hind leg lifted up so the cubs could nurse.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:05.330
The lions in the Serenera Valley have become so used to vehicles

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:08.630
that they barely raise their heads when we drive up to them,

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.870
though we were warned never to set foot out of the car

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:18.710
or they might suddenly turn ferocious at the unaccustomed sight of an unenclosed human.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:26.220
Soon we had our first glimpse of the unparalleled panorama of the vast Serengeti plains

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:29.780
being traversed by hundreds and thousands of hoofed animals.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:35.690
primarily zebras and wildebeest. At this time of year, it was not uncommon for grass fires to be

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:40.790
lit to burn off the dry grass and allow more nourishing new green shoots to sprout up.

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:45.910
Our van passed quite close to one line of fire, and we found that it was causing a number of

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:50.990
zebras and wildebeest to move on to get out of its way. The animals crossed the road in long lines

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:56.440
right in front of our vehicle and then galloped away to the left. Try counting just the small

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:59.880
herd of wildebeest filing past us here and remember

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.840
that the animals we are seeing are only scattered remnants of the incredible numbers of wildebeest

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:10.380
which every year take part in the spectacular mass migration for which Serengeti is most famous.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:15.760
This generally takes place in May or June when as many as half a million of these animals start

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:20.860
the long overland trek which will take them north and west to where permanent water is available

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:25.620
during the dry season. Then in November and December they return to these short grass plains to have

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:29.660
their calves but even these remaining summer animals made a tremendous

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:29.980
impression

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:34.360
on us as we watch them disappear in the dust under a sunset sky.

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:38.660
The Lobo Wildlife Lodge, where we spent the next night,

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:42.300
is a boldly designed architectural masterpiece

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.640
located some 40 kilometers north of Serenera.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:50.400
It is built right into the gaps and contours of a massive rock promontory

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.320
overlooking another part of the Serengeti Plains.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:57.340
We were absolutely awestruck walking around the many-level structure,

00:27:57.340 --> 00:27:59.860
which includes on top of the rocks an outdoor

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:07.660
swimming pool with a magnificent view out over the plains. Comfortable sleeping quarters were

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:13.180
provided in these three-story dormitories with every picture window room having its own

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:19.600
incomparable view. The next morning our extended Serengeti game drive continued and at times we

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:24.640
could see zebra and wildebeest covering the landscape in all directions as far as the eye

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:28.380
could see. This was truly the Serengeti experience.

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:33.690
At one stopping place, we were able to film a large saddle-billed stork

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:36.990
flying gracefully off with its great white wings flapping

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:40.070
and then gliding down to a new landing spot.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:46.040
The beautiful Impala are a thrill to watch as they bound high into the air,

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:47.900
leaping over a small ravine.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:52.280
Following at the rear of this herd of females is their male master

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:54.080
with his handsome set of horns.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:59.900
At noon on this great day, we stopped for a picnic lunch at the northern...

00:28:59.900 --> 00:28:59.920
Thank you.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.080
gatehouse to the park, not far from the border with Kenya. Our tour group was fortunate to have

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:10.820
outstanding leaders. The tour director was a young man named Jim Heck, who had lived in East Africa

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:15.500
himself for a number of years and had developed an excellent tourist operation known as Morgan

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:22.160
Tours, with many key contacts of people and places. Dr. Donald Bruning was our traveling expert from

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:28.040
the New York Zoological Society. He was the zoo's curator of ornithology, whose tremendous knowledge

00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:29.920
about birds was only matched by his

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.360
personal warmth and enthusiasm. Of course, everyone had their own camera, but our group had

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:40.960
the extra benefit of an experienced professional photographer named Ken Bertrand, whose superb

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:45.860
snatch shots provided a welcome supplement for the countless personal photos taken by

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:51.440
Meg and the other tour members. On our way back to Serenera, we were able to observe another

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:57.970
species of antelope known as the water buck, and again, only the males have horns. These deer-like

00:29:57.970 --> 00:29:59.850
animals usually there are supplied

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.580
of water. We counted 23 of them making up this peaceful group in an open clearing. At sunrise

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:14.440
the next day, we went on an early morning game drive and were rewarded by seeing a quarry bustard.

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:22.150
These large, unusual birds look like a cross between a turkey and an ostrich. Although they

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:27.270
are able to fly, they spend most of their time stalking around on the ground, looking for mice,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.830
snakes, and locusts to eat.

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:38.390
another very populous african bird of prey is the marabou stork before leaving serenera meg

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:44.010
wanted to get a close-up photo of these large long-beaked birds three of whom seemed to have

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:50.410
taken up residence in front of the lodge after a slow respectful approach meg finally got them to

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:59.900
pose motionless for a neat group portrait we had mixed emotions when we finally departed the serengeti

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:03.550
but there was one additional Tanzanian park which we wanted to see.

00:31:03.830 --> 00:31:06.490
My favorite vantage point when riding in the van

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:09.550
was standing in the midsection with my head above the roof opening

00:31:09.550 --> 00:31:11.330
so I could see in all directions

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:15.890
and call out the Swahili greeting of Jumbo to anyone we met.

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:22.400
The Arusha National Park, with entrance gate signs in both English and Swahili,

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:25.880
was a much smaller park, but exceedingly lush

00:31:25.880 --> 00:31:29.220
with green rainforest trees covering the hillsides.

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:36.860
it was here that we were to experience the single most exciting wildlife adventure of our entire

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:45.730
trip but first let's exchange viewpoints with a giraffe this giraffe is being looked

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:50.930
at by a tourist who looks like this to the giraffe while the giraffe looks like this to the tourist

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:59.610
the arusha park is noted for its distinctive populations of giraffe and elephants as we were

00:31:59.610 --> 00:31:59.850
watching

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:06.870
filming this large group of giraffe our van suddenly came to a stop and we saw that there

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:12.150
was a young elephant right in the middle of the road up ahead of us off to the right there was

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:17.220
a small herd of elephants but the calf was blocking our way one of the other minibuses

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:22.800
was directly in front of us after waiting quite a while the first bus started moving slowly ahead

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:29.970
but when it did so a large elephant with only one tusk moved out onto the road

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.380
between us and the calf. It started flapping its ears and shaking its head as if threatening to

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:41.110
charge us. Our drivers immediately backed up, not wanting to challenge a mother protecting her calf.

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:46.570
Finally, after several near charges, the calf did move off to the right and the large elephant

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:50.990
moved into the brush on the left of the road. But then when the vans drove forward again,

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:56.070
passing between them, the giant female with one tusk emerged from the brush right beside our van,

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:57.790
trumpeting wildly as we rushed past.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:09.490
That was truly a breathtaking encounter.

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:13.300
As if to reassure us on our last day in Tanzania,

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:18.300
Mount Kilimanjaro suddenly appeared from among the clouds on our way out of the park,

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:22.040
giving us our first view of its magnificent snow-covered peak

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:25.460
towering in the distance nearly 20,000 feet high.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:29.020
It was a fitting postlude for the first part of our trip

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:29.980
and a dramatic trek.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:33.940
for the further adventures awaiting us when we flew north to the country of Kenya.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:37.980
Like Tanzania, Kenya is comparable in size to the state of Texas.

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:40.880
It lies right on the equator, and during our two weeks there,

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.300
we visited eight of its great national parks.

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:45.660
We started in the capital city of Nairobi,

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:48.800
where there is this monumental statue of Jomo Kenyatta,

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:52.640
the heroic native figure who became the first president of Kenya

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:56.240
when the country won its independence from Britain in 1964.

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:59.980
This is the 28-story Kenyatta Conference Center.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.180
center in the heart of Nairobi on the top floor of which is a revolving restaurant.

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:09.920
From this vantage point one gets a panoramic view of both the rural outskirts and the crowded

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:14.660
urban center of this bustling cosmopolitan city of more than half a million people.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:20.440
It has many high-rise hotels, office buildings, and apartment houses with attractive stores

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:21.540
and tree-lined streets.

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:24.180
We found it a fascinating mixture of old and new.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:29.740
Our overnight accommodations in Nairobi were at the world-famous New Stanley Hotel, said

00:34:29.740 --> 00:34:29.980
to be

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.480
the crossroads of continents, where travelers from around the globe exchanged messages posted

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:41.220
on the celebrated Thorn Tree message board right in the middle of the sidewalk cafe. Meg was

00:34:41.220 --> 00:34:45.340
surprised to find a personal note for herself there, which turned out to be from her husband

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:52.930
inviting her to tea on the terrace. In Kenya, our group had a new set of minivans and drivers

00:34:52.930 --> 00:34:59.970
for our safari tours. Our first stop was at the Nairobi Park Animal Orphan.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.900
where I was able to film behind a fenced enclosure a mother leopard being kept

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:10.400
there for reasons of health on two occasions on our trip we did see wild

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:14.240
leopards but it was not possible for me to photograph them with their brilliant

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:22.460
colors they are beautiful creatures within just a few miles of Nairobi we

00:35:22.680 --> 00:35:28.720
encountered this wild rhino wandering across the road then we headed down the

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:29.920
highway toward the mass.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.170
Ambocelli Game Reserve. Along the way we sighted a distant pair of giraffes, the first we had seen

00:35:36.170 --> 00:35:44.980
in Kenya, and a few scattered wildebeest. We got a good close-up view of a male ostrich, the only

00:35:44.980 --> 00:35:50.880
flightless bird native to Africa. The ostrich is the largest bird in the world, and though it can't

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:58.700
fly, it can really run. We also came across a fine specimen of the silver-backed jackal. These

00:35:58.700 --> 00:35:59.980
These handsome scavengers have

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:11.160
a high-pitched bark which sounds like this would you mind stopping for a moment so we can get a

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:20.000
picture that's fine thank you very much now take a look at these two warthogs as they tear across

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:27.340
the road with their tails straight up the ambuseli lodge where we were to spend the next two nights

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:29.860
was a spread-out set of buildings and cottages

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:37.960
on a flat open area of ground without much shade the larger main structure housed the dining room

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:48.000
and a comfortable lounge and outdoor patio lunchtime we enjoyed the company of a pair of

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:55.260
superb starlings showing off their glossy iridescent plumage as with all our game

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:59.800
drives each tour member was assigned to a designated vehicle with a particular

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:06.760
driver. Many hours of close company riding by the five passengers and the skilled driver who was on

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:13.940
the front right side created a strong bond among us. The Kenyan driver for our van was Joseph

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:20.440
Kimathi and he had a very warm personality as well as being extremely knowledgeable about wildlife and

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:26.920
an excellent driver. The remarkable thing about this scene was not the small pride of lions among

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:29.460
the bushes beside this carcass of a zebra.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.000
but rather the incredible number of tourists it had attracted.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:38.560
It was almost completely surrounded by a total of nine minibuses.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:42.000
We all agreed that this was too much.

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.950
This old baboon didn't seem to think too highly of it either.

00:37:47.750 --> 00:37:52.290
Behind him, a whole troop of baboons was making its way across an open field,

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:55.470
including males, females, and lots of babies.

00:37:56.320 --> 00:37:59.440
And then came Ambecelli's elephants.

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:06.190
believe me this experience was unforgettable driving down among some tall bushes we found

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:11.930
ourselves right in the midst of a whole herd of elephants most of them taller than the tops of our

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:18.920
vans and almost close enough to touch they were of all sizes and on all sides as everyone kept

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:26.300
taking picture after incredible picture one large female again with just one tusk stepped

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:29.980
out in front and faced our way with a white egret on the ground.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.580
before her. She clearly was issuing us a warning with her body movements and then a shrill

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:48.240
trumpeting. We were somewhat relieved when, subsequently, the entire herd filed silently

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:59.890
by right beside our van in a patient line. The one animal on our Big Ten list which we had not

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.880
yet seen was the cheetah. But in one corner of the reserve, our drivers finally found a small

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:11.220
group of cheetah sitting quietly on their haunches or lying on the ground in the shadows of bushes

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:17.120
and trees. Their peaceful appearance belied their reputation as the fastest animal on earth,

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:23.170
having been clocked chasing antelope at speeds of up to 60 miles an hour. Once again, we were warned

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:28.550
not to get out of the vans, but we were mesmerized by the sight of these beautiful creatures with

00:39:28.550 --> 00:39:29.650
their small heads.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.240
and long, lean bodies and legs.

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:36.060
They moved and sat with such grace and dignity,

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:38.900
demonstrating their imperial disdain

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:41.620
for the human interlopers in the big painted buses.

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:45.430
Finally, we bid the cheetahs farewell,

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:49.350
leaving their royal majesties to reign in peace

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:51.490
over their own natural kingdom.

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:56.920
Back at the lodge for lunch, we were treated

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:59.560
to a colorful display by the remarkable bird

00:39:59.560 --> 00:39:59.740
identify

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.830
as the Upupa Epopas. It looked like a small hammerhead woodpecker, and its profile was as

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:16.180
striking as its name. On our afternoon game drive that day, we had close seats for an unexpected

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:23.050
demonstration of lion natural behavior. In an open field, we saw two males and one lioness.

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:29.590
As we observed them coming together, it became clear which was the dominant male. With one male

00:40:29.590 --> 00:40:29.870
expert

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:37.000
from the grassy boudoir, we didn't have to wait long for the next occurrence. Almost before we

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:44.160
knew what was happening, the dominant male was behind the female, mounting her and mating. The

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:49.360
female remained passively lying on the ground while the male continued his unresisted activity

00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:54.540
right there in front of us, totally ignoring the presence of our vans. Finally, the male nipped the

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:59.970
nape of the female's neck. She snarled and rolled over, and he disengaged.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:06.500
engaged himself and walked off. Viewing this was certainly an eye-opening experience.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:14.720
A short time later, we came upon a beautiful family scene with a mother lioness lying on the

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:21.320
ground, peacefully surveying the scene with three young cubs lolling around her. The cubs

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:27.730
also seemed unperturbed by the visiting tourists, and one walked over so close that a telephoto lens

00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:29.160
would have been useless.

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:44.440
in the bushes nearby was the father lion just giving an occasional yawn further on it was a

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:52.690
joy to see two beautifully horned male impella and then we had an encounter with a species

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:59.780
of antelope we had not seen before the tiny dick dick the smallest of the antelopes there were two

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:05.710
them in an open clearing, and the smaller one walked right over to where our van was,

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:16.360
looked up at us with its large, luminous eye, and proceeded to defecate, dropping a stream

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:20.960
of little black pellets to mark the border of its territory, and let us know we were not

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:26.000
to trespass beyond it. It was a very effective means of communication for so small a creature.

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:29.970
At the other end of the size scale, the next day,

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.360
we witnessed a magnificent parade of elephants making their way through the bush to a new

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:37.480
feeding location.

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:44.220
This huge group included animals at all stages of development, from very young to very old,

00:42:44.500 --> 00:42:48.240
but no doubt all connected with family ties of one kind or another.

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:54.400
What a marvelous sight this was as the whole herd moved by with Mount Kilimanjaro hidden

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:59.780
behind the clouds in the background, in the heart of elephant territory.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:07.420
in the Amboseli game range, we enjoyed the unique privilege of visiting the long-term campsite

00:43:07.420 --> 00:43:13.280
of wildlife researcher and author Cynthia Moss, whose famous book about animal behavior in East

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:18.460
Africa I had brought with me. Cynthia was living here in a couple of large tents while completing

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:24.710
a four-year study of the over 400 elephant residents of Amboseli. She had compiled a mammoth

00:43:24.710 --> 00:43:29.910
photo directory with pictures and descriptions of each individual elephant, which she shared with us.

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:33.580
While listening enthralled to her first-hand account of her fascinating work,

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:38.060
we looked up and saw a huge bull tusker, whose name she said was Patrick,

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:40.500
sauntered by in the adjacent field.

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:45.230
Her field studies were being supported by the New York Zoological Society.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:50.710
I was greatly impressed by the tremendous size of this elephant jawbone

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:53.010
and its vegetarian-style teeth.

00:43:55.370 --> 00:43:59.880
We learned that African elephants live in a matriarchal society.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.600
and this was clearly evident in the extended family group which crossed our

00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:09.860
roadway when we were leaving the park. It was made up of adult females and young

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:15.150
and a number of small calves. The mature females appeared to be acting as

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:19.450
crossing guards trying to keep the calves surrounded by larger elephants.

00:44:20.290 --> 00:44:24.490
Tragically, despite strong conservation efforts by the governments of most East

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:28.490
African countries, Africa's elephant populations have been declining

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:29.970
drastically in recent years.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:34.720
from several million originally to a new estimated total of less than one million.

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:43.060
They were being poached for their ivory and their habitat was being decreased by encroachments due to the growth in human populations.

00:44:44.380 --> 00:44:49.180
When the oldest and largest matriarch in this group finally appeared, bringing up the rear,

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:58.260
we hoped that Cynthia's research would help East Africa find new ways to assure the long-term survival of these magnificent animals for generations to come.

00:44:58.260 --> 00:44:59.970
one striking aspect

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:06.160
of Kenyan society was its respect for extreme differences in culture and lifestyle. We had seen

00:45:06.160 --> 00:45:11.360
western-like homes, businesses and clothes in the urban setting of Nairobi. Now, en route to the next

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:16.940
wildlife area, we were introduced to a rural boma with small clay huts surrounded by a protective

00:45:16.940 --> 00:45:21.960
thornbush barrier. For a small fee, we were allowed to enter and photograph these Maasai

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:27.400
tribespeople in their brightly colored garb while the children chanted a song to entertain us.

00:45:27.400 --> 00:45:29.660
their nomadic cattle-tending lifestyle.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.720
had remained essentially unchanged for centuries.

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:38.120
Africans make up more than 98% of Kenya's population of 15 million people,

00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:40.500
and they include many different native tribes.

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:44.200
The government's policy has been one of seeking peaceful coexistence

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:46.600
for all of the country's contrasting cultures.

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:52.110
The Tsavo National Park is the oldest national park in East Africa,

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:54.010
having been created in 1948.

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:59.970
Near its northwest border, there's a hillside covered with a massive lava flow of relative...

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:05.440
recent origin. The Great Rift Valley runs through Kenya and evidence of past

00:46:05.440 --> 00:46:10.580
volcanic eruptions were widespread in this area. Our main destination this day

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:14.820
however after passing through the symbolic Rhino entrance gate was the

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:21.380
fabulous Kilaguni Lodge. Dating back to 1952 this was the first National Park

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:25.600
Lodge designed and built for the express purpose of providing the best possible

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:29.780
wildlife viewing for tourists. The stone-walled veranda overlooked

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.440
an active waterhole with an incomparable background of plains, trees, hills, and mountains,

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:41.340
which was frequented day and night by all kinds of animals and birds. Our favorite mealtime

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:46.560
visitor was a red-billed hornbill who had no hesitancy about picking up tidbits from

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:53.190
the veranda wall and even from our outdoor dining tables. What a fantastic experience it

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:59.410
was just to sit and look out over this quintessential African landscape, even the ungainly marabou

00:46:59.410 --> 00:46:59.850
stored.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:06.080
became a thing of beauty when it took to the air, spread its giant wings, and soared into the boundless sky.

00:47:07.750 --> 00:47:14.720
Tsavo was perhaps most famous for its elephants, which included lone bulls like this one, as well as large family herds.

00:47:14.900 --> 00:47:19.480
It was also not uncommon to see extensive herds of Cape Buffalo here,

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:24.620
in this largest of African national parks embracing more than 8,000 square miles.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:29.970
One favorite stamping ground for both elephants and hippos was the famous museum.

00:47:30.740 --> 00:47:35.620
Here we were able to get out of our vans and walk down to the edge of a large pond.

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:40.390
In the shallow water on the other side of the pond was a mind-boggling assemblage

00:47:40.790 --> 00:47:46.030
of dust-coated elephants who had just come down to the springs for a drink, shower, and bath.

00:47:46.370 --> 00:47:51.470
Just standing there so close, watching them spray and dust each other with their hose-like trunks

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:53.210
was an unforgettable experience.

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:57.990
In another part of the pond, there were several submerged hippos.

00:47:57.990 --> 00:47:59.890
All we could see of them were their eyes.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.720
nostrils, ears, and backs when they occasionally broke the surface to breathe, but the name

00:48:04.720 --> 00:48:11.220
hippopotamus does mean river horse. Leaving Savo, we headed north to visit more Kenyan parks,

00:48:11.400 --> 00:48:16.800
passing on the way a different species of zebra with much narrower stripes. These were called

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:22.680
Grevy's zebra, and they are found only in northern Kenya. Not far from a picturesque waterfall,

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:29.970
we got ready to board the Ark. This unique game-viewing lodge is in the shape of

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:35.420
a large boat built on an elevated promontory right in the heart of the forest in abradera

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:43.040
national park after entering by way of a catwalk two by two we found that below the broad bow of

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:50.290
this great wooden ship was a salt lick and marsh which is visited day and night by a large variety

00:48:50.290 --> 00:48:59.170
of animals we were too fascinated watching the wildlife in the illuminated oasis below us like

00:48:59.170 --> 00:48:59.970
this pair of large

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:08.580
rhinoceros to get much sleep that night in our assigned staterooms. On our way

00:49:08.580 --> 00:49:14.310
out the next morning we admired the great beauty and grace of these golden

00:49:14.430 --> 00:49:22.470
crowned cranes showing off their feathered finery. Driving further north

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:27.150
to our next destination we crossed the equator and we all wanted to have the

00:49:27.150 --> 00:49:29.980
group's picture taken at the crossing sign to prove

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:39.850
that we'd been there. The Samburu Lodge, lying along the banks of a northern jungle river,

00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:47.200
shaded by large tropical trees, had a storied African atmosphere to it. We spent two nights

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:52.080
here, and the outdoor terrace was a dramatic sight from which to look for crocodiles along

00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:59.970
the banks or watch an elephant warily ford the shallow stream. On our game day,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:06.320
drives in the Sambura Reserve, we added several new species like this reticulated giraffe to our

00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:13.840
filmed trophies list. One of the most interesting new species was the gerinook, known as the giraffe

00:50:13.840 --> 00:50:19.380
gazelle. It has a very long neck and often stands on its hind legs to eat leaves from the higher

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:27.580
branches of the thorn bushes. We also were fortunate to see a large herd of oryx, a beautiful

00:50:27.580 --> 00:50:29.900
endangered antelope with

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:29.940
Thank you very much.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.930
long straight horns the markings on the reticulated species of giraffe here have a much clearer sharper

00:50:37.930 --> 00:50:42.950
pattern in contrast to the irregular fig leaf splatches on this maasai giraffe seen earlier

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:48.750
white bordered rectangular patches like those shown here distinguish the reticulated giraffe

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:55.880
found only in the north on the last leg of our trip heading back south we enjoyed lunch in the

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:59.240
cooling mist of the tall thompson's falls and

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.840
then stopped at the famous Lake Nakuru National Park to view the incredible array of bird

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:10.980
life which covered its shores. Flamingos predominated, but there were also pelicans,

00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:18.910
cormorants, ibis, storks, and countless others. Before returning to Nairobi for our plane flight

00:51:18.910 --> 00:51:24.710
home from Africa, this time passing green cultivated farmlands, as our many bus drivers

00:51:24.710 --> 00:51:29.970
showed their great skill in maneuvering over rough terrain, we had one more special

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:40.810
African safari experience. We spent two nights at the Masai Mara Buffalo Camp, sleeping on large

00:51:40.810 --> 00:51:48.980
platform tents under thatched roofs right beside the Mara River. A friendly mongoose was the camp

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:59.970
mascot emerging from our tent under the thatched roof in the morning.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:06.020
morning, walking to the main house for breakfast, we observed in the rushing river alongside

00:52:06.020 --> 00:52:13.650
us a hippo, though all we could see were its nostrils. Later, on a foot safari, we found

00:52:13.650 --> 00:52:20.080
both the hippo's swimming pool and their lounging beach. Kenya's first president, Jomo Kenyatta,

00:52:20.310 --> 00:52:26.380
called his country's wildlife a priceless heritage for the future. Tourism provides

00:52:26.380 --> 00:52:29.970
It's essential income for preserving it and viewing the game on.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:35.300
these vast plains for the last time, we hoped that our visit had helped toward their conservation

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:47.580
for future generations. The Swahili phrase for beautiful is Missouri sauna. After a farewell

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:53.480
dinner at the Masai Kikoro Lodge, we all expressed our joy and gratitude with these Swahili words.
